Below is a compact, self‑contained C++ implementation of **binary search** that works on any sorted array (or vector) of comparable elements.  
It is written as a **template** so you can use it with `int`, `double`, `std::string`, or any type that supports `operator<` and `operator==`.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   Binary search – iterative version
   -------------------------------------------------------------
   Parameters
   ----------
   arr : const std::vector<T>&
       Sorted container (ascending order).
   target : const T&
       Value to look for.

   Returns
   -------
   int
       Index of the target if found; otherwise –1.
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
template <typename T>
int binarySearch(const std::vector<T>& arr, const T& target)
{
    int left  = 0;
    int right = static_cast<int>(arr.size()) - 1;

    while (left <= right)
    {
        // Avoid overflow: mid = left + (right-left)/2
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target)          // Found
            return mid;
        else if (arr[mid] < target)      // Search right half
            left = mid + 1;
        else                             // Search left half
            right = mid - 1;
    }

    return -1;   // Not found
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   Binary search – recursive version
   -------------------------------------------------------------
   Parameters
   ----------
   arr : const std::vector<T>&
       Sorted container (ascending order).
   target : const T&
       Value to look for.
   left : int
       Left boundary of the current search interval.
   right : int
       Right boundary of the current search interval.

   Returns
   -------
   int
       Index of the target if found; otherwise –1.
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
template <typename T>
int binarySearchRecursive(const std::vector<T>& arr,
                          const T& target,
                          int left,
                          int right)
{
    if (left > right)
        return -1;   // Not found

    int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

    if (arr[mid] == target)
        return mid;
    else if (arr[mid] < target)
        return binarySearchRecursive(arr, target, mid + 1, right);
    else
        return binarySearchRecursive(arr, target, left, mid - 1);
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   Convenience wrapper for the recursive version
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
template <typename T>
int binarySearchRecursive(const std::vector<T>& arr, const T& target)
{
    return binarySearchRecursive(arr, target, 0,
                                 static_cast<int>(arr.size()) - 1);
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   Example usage
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
int main()
{
    std::vector<int> data = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15};

    int target = 9;
    int idxIter = binarySearch(data, target);
    int idxRec  = binarySearchRecursive(data, target);

    std::cout << "Iterative: index = " << idxIter << '\n';
    std::cout << "Recursive: index = " << idxRec  << '\n';

    // Search for a value that is not present
    target = 8;
    std::cout << "Not found (iterative): index = "
              << binarySearch(data, target) << '\n';

    return 0;
}
```

### How it works

1. **Iterative version**  
   * Keeps two indices, `left` and `right`, that bound the current search interval.  
   * Repeatedly calculates the middle index `mid`.  
   * If the middle element equals the target, the index is returned.  
   * If the middle element is smaller, the search continues in the right half (`left = mid + 1`).  
   * Otherwise, the search continues in the left half (`right = mid - 1`).  
   * If the loop exits without finding the target, `-1` is returned.

2. **Recursive version**  
   * The same logic is applied, but the function calls itself with a narrowed interval.  
   * The wrapper `binarySearchRecursive(const std::vector<T>&, const T&)` hides the `left`/`right` parameters from the caller.

### Complexity

* **Time** – `O(log n)` in the worst case.  
* **Space** – `O(1)` for the iterative version; `O(log n)` stack space for the recursive version.

Feel free to drop the function into any project that needs a fast lookup in a sorted array!

Here are two standard implementations: **Iterative** (preferred for production due to $O(1)$ space complexity) and **Recursive** (cleaner logic, but uses $O(\log n)$ stack space).

Both are written as **templates** to work with any comparable type (`int`, `double`, `string`, custom structs with `operator<`, etc.).

### 1. Iterative Implementation (Recommended)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm> // For std::sort (in example)

// Template function for generic types
// Returns index of target if found, otherwise -1
template <typename T>
int binarySearch(const std::vector<T>& arr, const T& target) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = static_cast<int>(arr.size()) - 1;

    while (left <= right) {
        // Prevents potential integer overflow for massive arrays compared to (left + right) / 2
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            return mid; // Found
        } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
            left = mid + 1; // Search right half
        } else {
            right = mid - 1; // Search left half
        }
    }
    return -1; // Not found
}
```

### 2. Recursive Implementation

```cpp
// Helper function for recursion (hides the left/right indices from the caller)
template <typename T>
int binarySearchRecursiveHelper(const std::vector<T>& arr, const T& target, int left, int right) {
    if (left > right) return -1; // Base case: not found

    int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

    if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
    if (arr[mid] < target) 
        return binarySearchRecursiveHelper(arr, target, mid + 1, right);
    else 
        return binarySearchRecursiveHelper(arr, target, left, mid - 1);
}

// Wrapper function for cleaner API
template <typename T>
int binarySearchRecursive(const std::vector<T>& arr, const T& target) {
    return binarySearchRecursiveHelper(arr, target, 0, static_cast<int>(arr.size()) - 1);
}
```

