Below is a compact, self‑contained C++ implementation that finds the **Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA)** of two nodes in a **Binary Search Tree (BST)**.  
The code includes:

* A minimal `TreeNode` definition.  
* A recursive and an iterative version of the LCA routine.  
* A small demo that builds a BST, picks two nodes, and prints the LCA.  
* Complexity analysis and a few edge‑case notes.

```cpp
/*********************************************************************
 *  Lowest Common Ancestor in a Binary Search Tree (BST)
 *
 *  Complexity:
 *      Time   : O(h)   (h = height of the tree, O(log n) for a balanced BST)
 *      Space  : O(1)   (iterative)  or O(h) (recursive stack)
 *
 *  Assumptions:
 *      - The tree is a proper BST (left < node < right).
 *      - Both target nodes p and q exist in the tree.
 *      - TreeNode::val is unique (or at least the BST property holds).
 *********************************************************************/

#include <iostream>
#include <memory>   // for std::unique_ptr (optional)
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm> // for std::min, std::max

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Basic BST node definition
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
struct TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode* left;
    TreeNode* right;
    TreeNode(int x) : val(x), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
};

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Recursive LCA implementation
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
TreeNode* lowestCommonAncestorRecursive(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {
    if (!root) return nullptr;          // safety guard

    // If both p and q are smaller than root, LCA lies in left subtree
    if (p->val < root->val && q->val < root->val)
        return lowestCommonAncestorRecursive(root->left, p, q);

    // If both are larger, LCA lies in right subtree
    if (p->val > root->val && q->val > root->val)
        return lowestCommonAncestorRecursive(root->right, p, q);

    // Otherwise root is the split point (or one of p/q equals root)
    return root;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Iterative LCA implementation
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
TreeNode* lowestCommonAncestorIterative(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {
    while (root) {
        if (p->val < root->val && q->val < root->val) {
            root = root->left;          // both on left
        } else if (p->val > root->val && q->val > root->val) {
            root = root->right;         // both on right
        } else {
            return root;                // split point
        }
    }
    return nullptr; // should never happen if p and q exist
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper: insert a value into a BST (used for demo)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
TreeNode* insert(TreeNode* root, int val) {
    if (!root) return new TreeNode(val);
    if (val < root->val) root->left = insert(root->left, val);
    else                 root->right = insert(root->right, val);
    return root;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Demo: build a BST, pick two nodes, find LCA
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
int main() {
    /* Construct the following BST
              20
             /  \
            10   30
           / \   / \
          5  15 25 35
    */
    std::vector<int> values = {20, 10, 30, 5, 15, 25, 35};
    TreeNode* root = nullptr;
    for (int v : values) root = insert(root, v);

    // Pick two nodes (by value)
    TreeNode* p = root->left->right;   // node with value 15
    TreeNode* q = root->right->left;   // node with value 25

    TreeNode* lcaRec = lowestCommonAncestorRecursive(root, p, q);
    TreeNode* lcaItr = lowestCommonAncestorIterative(root, p, q);

    std::cout << "Recursive LCA of " << p->val << " and " << q->val
              << " is: " << (lcaRec ? std::to_string(lcaRec->val) : "null") << '\n';

    std::cout << "Iterative LCA of " << p->val << " and " << q->val
              << " is: " << (lcaItr ? std::to_string(lcaItr->val) : "null") << '\n';

    // Clean up (optional in short demo)
    // In production code, consider smart pointers or a proper destructor.
    return 0;
}
```

### How it works

1. **BST property**  
   For any node `root`, all values in the left subtree are `< root->val` and all values in the right subtree are `> root->val`.

2. **Finding the split point**  
   * If both target nodes are on the same side of `root`, the LCA must be deeper on that side.  
   * If they lie on opposite sides (or one equals `root`), `root` is the lowest node that separates them – the LCA.

3. **Recursive vs. Iterative**  
   * The recursive version follows the same logic but uses the call stack.  
   * The iterative version simply walks down the tree, updating `root` until the split point is found.

### Edge Cases

* **One node is ancestor of the other** – the ancestor node is returned because the split condition is satisfied when one of `p` or `q` equals `root`.
* **Both nodes are the same** – the node itself is returned.
* **Tree is empty** – the functions return `nullptr` (though the problem statement usually guarantees that `p` and `q` exist).

